{"id": "12508dcc-0495-4bda-bc94-41dfdc0e720c", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "This is a classic symptom of **time drift on a long-stopped EC2 instance**. Here's what's happening:

When an EC2 instance is stopped for months, its hardware clock freezes. On restart, the instance boots with the **system clock set to the time it was stopped** (months in the past) before the chrony/NTP service has had a chance to sync it. If curl/yum tries to validate TLS certificates on the RHUI repo endpoints *before* the clock catches up, the client sees the repo's certificate as "not yet valid" or compares against its expiry incorrectly — surfacing as "certificate has expired" even though the cert itself is fine. This isn't a repo or GPG key problem; it's your instance's local clock lying about the date.

